#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Regression tests for the subject-tagging fix in
article_picture_pass.extract_article_entities
(headline-subject promotion + descriptor-prefix trim).

Built from the five real articles the 2026-10-30 inline
dry run found with true subjects mis-tagged as body
mentions (fixtures: headline + body as published, from
inline-subjects-only-dryrun-2026-10-30-data.json and the
live article records):
  Rafale Marine — "Rafale Marine Lands on INS Vikrant…"
  Yuvraj Singh — "Yuvraj Singh Says India Must Give…"
  Josh Inglis — "Josh Inglis Makes Australian History…"
  Eric Schmidt — extracted as the descriptor-prefixed
                 "Former Google CEO Eric Schmidt", the
                 article's ONLY entity, tagged body, and
                 invisible to headline_subject_names
  Statue of Unity — "Statue of Unity Rain Forest Opens…"

Root cause: step 1 tags a headline phrase only when the
body repeats it verbatim, and RES._PHRASE_RE matches
maximal capitalized runs — in title-case headlines the
run absorbs the verb ("Yuvraj Singh Says India Must"),
which the body never repeats, so the subject fell
through to body counting with the mention tag.

Run: python3 -u test_subject_tagging.py
"""
import os
import sys

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
sys.path.insert(0, HERE)

import article_picture_pass as P

FAILURES = []


def check(name, cond, detail=""):
    print(("  PASS " if cond else "  FAIL ") + name +
          (f" — {detail}" if detail and not cond else ""))
    if not cond:
        FAILURES.append(name)


ARTICLES = {'rafale': {'headline': 'Rafale Marine Lands on INS Vikrant on First Attempt in Carrier Trials', 'body': '<div class="key-takeaways"><ul><li>A Rafale Marine jet landed on INS Vikrant on its maiden attempt on October 29, the Indian Navy confirmed on October 30.</li><li>The trials are the first steps toward operationalising the aircraft, after shore-based tests at INS Hansa in Goa.</li><li>India signed an agreement with France on April 28, 2025, for 26 Rafale Marine jets worth about Rs 63,000 crore — 22 single-seat and four twin-seat.</li><li>Deliveries are expected to begin in late 2028 or 2029 and conclude by 2030-31; the jets will operate from INS Vikrant and INS Vikramaditya.</li></ul></div>\n<p>A Rafale Marine fighter aircraft successfully carried out an arrested landing on the Indian Navy\'s indigenous aircraft carrier INS Vikrant on October 29, completing the manoeuvre on its maiden attempt. The Indian Navy confirmed the milestone on October 30.</p>\n<p>&quot;A Rafale Marine aircraft successfully landed onboard Indian Navy\'s indigenous Aircraft Carrier INS Vikrant on its maiden attempt on 29 Oct 26. The ongoing trials mark the first steps towards operationalisation of the aircraft in the Indian Navy,&quot; the Navy said, according to <a href="https://www.ssbcrack.com/2026/10/rafale-marine-completes-maiden-landing-on-ins-vikrant-on-first-attempt.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SSB Crack</a>. The landing was conducted under the command of Captain Ashok Rao, commanding officer of INS Vikrant, and the Navy released video footage. <a href="https://www.indiasentinels.com/navy/indian-navy-rafale-marine-fighter-makes-maiden-landing-on-ins-vikrant-7677" target="_blank" rel="noopener">India Sentinels</a> also reported the first-attempt landing on Thursday.</p>\n<h2>From shore trials to the deck</h2>\n<p>The carrier landing followed shore-based trials at the Shore-Based Test Facility at INS Hansa in Goa. That facility replicates the ski-jump ramp and arrestor-wire systems used by Indian carriers and was used to assess the modifications required for the Rafale M to operate from INS Vikrant.</p>\n<p>Carrier trials of this kind check how an aircraft behaves on approach to the deck, in ski-jump launch, in arrested recovery and in handling on the flight deck, as well as how it works with the ship\'s arresting gear, India Sentinels noted. The Rafale M has a reinforced undercarriage and a tailhook for such landings.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/INS_Vikramaditya_%28in_Left%29_and_INS_Vikrant_%28in_Right%29.jpg/1920px-INS_Vikramaditya_%28in_Left%29_and_INS_Vikrant_%28in_Right%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail" alt="INS Vikrant. A Rafale Marine jet landed on INS Vikrant on its maiden attempt on October 29, the Indian Navy confirmed on October 30." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">INS Vikrant. A Rafale Marine jet landed on INS Vikrant on its maiden attempt on October 29, the Indian Navy confirmed on October 30.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>A technical mismatch the trials must prove out</h2>\n<p>The Rafale M was designed for Catapult Assisted Take-Off But Arrested Recovery (CATOBAR) operations, as used on the French Navy\'s carrier Charles de Gaulle. INS Vikrant uses Short Take-Off But Arrested Recovery (STOBAR): aircraft take off from a ski-jump ramp and are recovered using arrestor wires. The successful maiden landing validates the aircraft\'s compatibility with the STOBAR system after the necessary assessments, SSB Crack reported.</p>\n<p>One practical constraint remains the jet\'s fixed, non-folding wings, with a span of about 10.9 metres — wider than Vikrant\'s aircraft lifts at roughly 10 metres, which were designed for the folding-wing MiG-29K. Work is under way to modify the lifts, and procedures may include temporarily removing wingtip pylons during transfers between flight deck and hangar. Similar constraints exist on INS Vikramaditya.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Dassault_Rafale_Marine_%2835121073660%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original" alt="Dassault Rafale Marine. A Rafale Marine jet landed on INS Vikrant on its maiden attempt on October 29, the Indian Navy confirmed on October 30." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">Dassault Rafale Marine. A Rafale Marine jet landed on INS Vikrant on its maiden attempt on October 29, the Indian Navy confirmed on October 30.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>The Rs 63,000 crore acquisition behind it</h2>\n<p>India and France signed an intergovernmental agreement on April 28, 2025, for 26 Rafale Marine aircraft valued at approximately Rs 63,000 crore. The package comprises 22 single-seat fighters and four twin-seat trainers, along with weapons, simulators, training and logistics support.</p>\n<p>The twin-engine Rafale M, built by Dassault Aviation, can carry out air defence, maritime strike, reconnaissance and deep-strike missions, with weapons including Meteor beyond-visual-range missiles, MICA missiles, SCALP cruise missiles and Exocet anti-ship missiles. Once inducted, it will operate alongside and eventually help replace the Navy\'s MiG-29K fleet, serving as an interim capability until the indigenous Twin-Engine Deck-Based Fighter becomes available. Commonality with the Indian Air Force\'s 36 Rafales should ease logistics and spares support.</p>\n<p>INS Vikrant, built by Cochin Shipyard and commissioned on September 2, 2022, displaces about 45,000 tonnes. A MiG-29K and the naval variant of the Light Combat Aircraft made its first fixed-wing landings on February 6, 2023; the Rafale M is now the third fixed-wing type to land on the ship.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>What happens next</h2>\n<p>Further trials will continue to establish the aircraft\'s full operational suitability for sustained carrier operations. Deliveries of India\'s own aircraft are expected to begin from late 2028 or 2029 and conclude by 2030-31, operating from both INS Vikrant and INS Vikramaditya. Until then, the MiG-29K remains the carriers\' embarked fighter.</p>\n<div class="related-coverage"><div class="related-coverage-title">Related coverage</div><a class="related-item" href="/articles/odisha-ganjam-floods-helicopters-airdrop-food-cyclone-arnab"><span class="related-item-title">Helicopters Airdrop Food as Cyclone Arnab Flooding Maroons Thousands in Odisha</span><span class="related-item-meta">News · 2026-10-30</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/rajnath-singh-india-battle-tested-expertise-asean-admm-plus-cebu"><span class="related-item-title">Rajnath Singh Offers India&#x27;s Battle-Tested Counter-Terror Expertise to ASEAN Partners</span><span class="related-item-meta">News · 2026-10-30</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/assam-minister-ulfa-remarks-sarma-clarifies-pro-talks-faction"><span class="related-item-title">Assam Minister&#x27;s ULFA Remarks Spark Row, Sarma Issues Clarification</span><span class="related-item-meta">News · 2026-10-30</span></a></div>', 'slug': 'rafale-marine-maiden-landing-ins-vikrant-first-attempt'}, 'yuvraj': {'headline': 'Yuvraj Singh Says India Must Give Young Batters ODI Chances', 'body': '<div class="key-takeaways"><ul><li>Former India all-rounder Yuvraj Singh says leaving Abhishek Sharma, Ishan Kishan and Vaibhav Sooryavanshi out of 50-over cricket does not do justice to their talent.</li><li>Yuvraj, a long-time mentor to Abhishek, spoke to PTI on the sidelines of the Protocol 32 launch event.</li><li>Kishan has played 32 ODIs, while Abhishek and Sooryavanshi have never been called up to India\'s ODI setup despite their T20I form.</li><li>Yuvraj conceded competition for places is fierce, but said the young batters\' performances should put them in the ODI squad soon.</li></ul></div>\n<p>Former India all-rounder Yuvraj Singh has said India are not doing justice to the talent of Abhishek Sharma, Ishan Kishan and Vaibhav Sooryavanshi by leaving them out of the one-day international setup, in comments reported by <a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/not-doing-justice-to-their-talent-yuvraj-singhs-massive-odi-verdict-on-abhishek-sharma-ishan-kishan-vaibhav-sooryavanshi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CricTracker</a> and <a href="https://crex.com/cricket-news/yuvraj-singh-wants-abhishek-sharma-vaibhav-sooryavanshi-to-play-odis-for-india-6ae4acf4525603a2aa219754" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CREX</a>.</p>\n<p>Yuvraj spoke to PTI on the sidelines of the Protocol 32 launch event. He has served as a long-time mentor to Abhishek, and his backing carried a direct message for the selectors ahead of India\'s five-match ODI series against New Zealand, which opens at Eden Park in Auckland on November 4.</p>\n<blockquote class="pull-quote"><p>"If we are not picking guys like Ishan Kishan, Abhishek and Vaibhav Sooryavanshi in 50-over cricket, then somewhere down the line we are not doing justice to their talent."</p><cite>— Yuvraj Singh, former India all-rounder</cite></blockquote>\n<h2>Three batters, three different cases</h2>\n<p>The three players Yuvraj named are at different stages. Kishan has already played 32 ODIs for India, and his most recent appearance in the format came on July 19, 2026, against England. Abhishek and Sooryavanshi, by contrast, have never been handed a call-up to the ODI setup, even though both have featured in India\'s recent T20I squad for the New Zealand tour.</p>\n<p>Abhishek\'s List A record gives Yuvraj\'s argument a statistical base: the opener has played 69 List A matches, scoring 2,196 runs at a strike rate of 100.18. Sooryavanshi\'s case rests on a rapid rise through age-group and senior cricket — he made his international debut in a T20I series in England, helped India U19 to the 2026 ICC U19 World Cup title in Zimbabwe, and holds one of the fastest List A centuries on record, an 84-ball 190 for Bihar.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Yuvraj_Singh_%286718443215%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original" alt="Yuvraj Singh (6718443215). Former India all-rounder Yuvraj Singh says leaving Abhishek Sharma, Ishan Kishan and Vaibhav Sooryavanshi out of 50-over cricket does not do justice to their talent." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">Yuvraj Singh (6718443215). Former India all-rounder Yuvraj Singh says leaving Abhishek Sharma, Ishan Kishan and Vaibhav Sooryavanshi out of 50-over cricket does not do justice to their talent.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>A mentor\'s backing, with a caveat</h2>\n<p>Yuvraj was careful to acknowledge the other side of the selection problem. India have a settled group of senior players, places in the ODI squad are limited, and breaking into the side is hard for any young batter. "I understand we have some great players and it is hard to make a spot for them. I think the way they are performing, they should be in the squad soon, hopefully," he said.</p>\n<p>He also placed Abhishek\'s rise on the player himself, saying whatever the opener has achieved is the result of his own hard work. That distinction matters coming from a mentor: Yuvraj is arguing for an opportunity, not claiming one is owed.</p>\n\n<h2>Why the timing matters</h2>\n<p>India are settling on a core group for the 2027 ICC Cricket World Cup, which makes every ODI squad from here a signal about who is inside the selectors\' long-term plans and who is outside them. Leaving three attacking left-hand options out of the 50-over picture entirely, Yuvraj argues, wastes the time those players need to learn the longer white-ball format at international level before a World Cup cycle peaks.</p>\n<p>For Indian fans in the diaspora following the New Zealand tour, the debate is a familiar one: a team rich in batting talent has to choose between continuity with proven ODI performers and early investment in the batters dominating T20 cricket. Yuvraj, a World Cup winner in 2011, has made his position plain — the talent is ready to be tested, and the testing ground has to be the ODI squad itself.</p>\n\n<div class="related-coverage"><div class="related-coverage-title">Related coverage</div><a class="related-item" href="/articles/abhishek-sharma-113-india-beat-new-zealand-3rd-t20i"><span class="related-item-title">Abhishek Sharma&#x27;s 113 Off 38 Balls Seals Series for India</span><span class="related-item-meta">Sports · 2026-10-27</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/eden-park-sold-out-india-new-zealand-t20i-record"><span class="related-item-title">Sold-Out Eden Park Crowd to Break New Zealand Bilateral Record for India T20I</span><span class="related-item-meta">Sports · 2026-10-30</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/ishan-kishan-ruled-out-wellington-t20i-recovering"><span class="related-item-title">Ishan Kishan Ruled Out of Wellington T20I Despite Recovering Well</span><span class="related-item-meta">Sports · 2026-10-26</span></a></div>', 'slug': 'yuvraj-singh-india-odi-chances-abhishek-kishan-sooryavanshi'}, 'schmidt': {'headline': 'Eric Schmidt Says AI Could Let Large Companies Cut 20% of Staff', 'body': '<div class="key-takeaways"><ul><li>Former Google CEO Eric Schmidt said AI could let large companies operate with about 20% fewer staff — roughly 2,000 roles in a 10,000-person company.</li><li>The roles at risk, in his account, are the \'glue people\': planning, coordination and moving information between teams. Sales, product building, leadership and work with regulators remain human jobs.</li><li>Schmidt, speaking to Norges Bank\'s Nicolai Tangen, also said Chinese open-weight AI models trail the US frontier by only about six months, and put true machine discovery — his AGI boundary — five to ten years away.</li><li>For readers in large-company coordination roles: Schmidt\'s advice to employers was to transition affected staff \'legally and humanely\'; he offered no timeline for when the cuts arrive.</li></ul></div>\n<p>Eric Schmidt ran Google for a decade, and he describes the job plainly: meetings, 12 hours a day, five days a week, plus weekends — about 63 hours of coordination. His argument now is that AI platforms can eliminate much of that \'coordination tax\', and with it a fifth of the workforce of a large company.</p>\n<p>Speaking on the In Good Company podcast with Nicolai Tangen, chief executive of Norges Bank Investment Management, Schmidt estimated that a 10,000-person company has, in his words, <a href="https://www.tokenpost.com/news/people/38769" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"a couple of thousand people who you don\'t need"</a> — and that leaders must find a legal, humane way to transition them out.</p>\n<blockquote class="pull-quote"><p>"Let\'s say you\'re running a 10,000-person company, my guess is you\'ve got a couple of thousand people who you don\'t need. And you better figure out a way to legally and humanely transition them, retire them out, or what have you."</p><cite>— Eric Schmidt, former CEO, Google</cite></blockquote>\n<h2>Which jobs, in his telling</h2>\n<p>Schmidt cited a friend whose fast-growing company cut 20% of its staff. The cuts fell on the \'glue people\' — those planning projects and moving numbers around — while salespeople, people who talk to regulators, and product builders stayed in demand. He said the friend received death threats over the layoffs. As <a href="https://jbiznews.com/ex-google-ceo-eric-schmidt-worked-60-hour-weeks-i-used-to-tell-people-my-job-is-to-sit-in-meetings-12-hours-a-day-he-says-ai-will-kill-that-burden/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">coverage of the interview</a> noted, Schmidt expects that pattern to become more common as AI agents take over gathering updates, passing them upward and keeping teams aligned.</p>\n<p>He was blunt about adoption: he holds a strong view that almost none of the companies listening will actually make the cultural changes required, even though agentic AI is, in his phrase, a powerful flattening of the organisation.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Eric_Schmidt%2C_Executive_Chairman%2C_Google_%28left%29_in_conversation_with_Nik_Gowing_%2811051254154%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original" alt="Power and Commerce in the Internet Age, 25 November 2013 cht.hm/1ixrhmP. Former Google CEO Eric Schmidt said AI could let large companies operate with about 20% fewer staff — roughly 2,000 roles in a 10,000-person company." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">Power and Commerce in the Internet Age, 25 November 2013 cht.hm/1ixrhmP. Former Google CEO Eric Schmidt said AI could let large companies operate with about 20% fewer staff — roughly 2,000 roles in a 10,000-person company.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>The wider AI picture</h2>\n<p>Schmidt rejected the idea that AI is a bubble, pointing to Anthropic\'s revenue growth — more than twice as fast as Google\'s at its peak, in his telling. His sharper warning was geopolitical: Chinese open-weight models trail the US frontier by only about six months, and because they are free, much of the world may end up running them. He put true machine discovery, his definition of the AGI boundary, five to ten years away, and said AI treaties are likely to arrive only after an accident.</p>\n<p>Schmidt\'s numbers are a prediction, not a plan announced by any employer. But they land in a year when large technology firms have repeatedly cut coordination and management layers while reporting record AI-driven revenue — which is why a former CEO\'s estimate, delivered on a sovereign wealth fund\'s podcast, is being read as a planning assumption rather than a provocation.</p>\n<div class="related-coverage"><div class="related-coverage-title">Related coverage</div><a class="related-item" href="/articles/google-gemini-4-carbon-coding-cybersecurity-model"><span class="related-item-title">Google Unveils Gemini 4 Carbon, Its Strongest Model for Coding and Cyber Defense</span><span class="related-item-meta">Technology · 2026-10-29</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/alphabet-earnings-miss-eps-2-12-revenue-127-billion-cloud-doubles"><span class="related-item-title">Alphabet Earnings Miss Estimates as EPS Falls to $2.12 Despite Revenue Rising 25% to $127.6 Billion</span><span class="related-item-meta">Markets Finance · 2026-10-29</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/bengaluru-tech-summit-2026-startups-investor-connect"><span class="related-item-title">Bengaluru Tech Summit 2026 Puts 1,000 Startups in Front of Global Investors</span><span class="related-item-meta">Technology · 2026-10-29</span></a></div>', 'slug': 'eric-schmidt-ai-cut-20-percent-jobs-large-companies'}, 'statue': {'headline': 'Statue of Unity Rain Forest Opens With Glass Skywalk, Artificial Rain', 'body': '<div class="key-takeaways"><ul><li>A new Rain Forest attraction at Ekta Nagar near the Statue of Unity is set to be inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi during his October 30-31 visit to Gujarat, according to a Gujarat Chief Minister\'s Office release.</li><li>The official release puts the cost at Rs 9.49 crore for 47,000 square metres, with a 220-metre glass-deck skywalk and a 5 to 8 metre waterfall; earlier reports put the cost at about Rs 12 crore over 12 acres, with 20 rain-gun towers for artificial rain.</li><li>The site brings together evergreen flora from the Western Ghats, the Himalayas and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, plus an information centre and a children\'s activity zone.</li><li>For diaspora visitors planning a trip: fly to Vadodara, 90 km from Kevadia, or take a direct train to Ekta Nagar station, about 5 km from the Statue of Unity entrance.</li></ul></div>\n<p>EKTA NAGAR, Gujarat — A rainforest built in Gujarat\'s Narmada district, complete with a glass skywalk above the canopy, an artificial waterfall and plants collected from across India, is set to open at Ekta Nagar, the township built around the Statue of Unity.</p>\n<p>Prime Minister Narendra Modi will inaugurate the project during his state visit on October 30-31, according to a press release issued by the Gujarat Chief Minister\'s Office on Thursday and reported by <a href="https://www.devdiscourse.com/article/politics/3998770-pm-modi-to-inaugurate-949-crore-rain-forest-with-220-metre-glass-skywalk-at-ekta-nagar" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Devdiscourse</a>. The attraction aims to boost eco-tourism around the Statue of Unity, the world\'s tallest statue.</p>\n<h2>What has been built</h2>\n<p>The official release describes a site spread across 47,000 square metres — about 11.6 acres — near the new Gora Bridge, close to the Vagadiya Checkpost, at an estimated cost of Rs 9.49 crore. Its centrepiece is a 220-metre-long glass-deck skywalk bridge, designed to give visitors a panoramic view of the forest canopy and the surrounding landscape from above.</p>\n<p>Earlier reporting on the same project described a larger headline figure and a different skywalk design. Reports citing the initial announcement put the project at about Rs 12 crore over 12 acres, with a circular glass skywalk about 11 metres above the ground and 22 metres in diameter, made of 132 curved, toughened laminated glass panels, each 35 millimetres thick. Those reports also described 20 rain-gun towers built to simulate monsoon rainfall and hold the temperature between 25 and 30 degrees Celsius, along with bubble fountains and waterfalls. The official release issued at inauguration does not mention the rain towers, and readers should treat its Rs 9.49 crore cost and 220-metre skywalk as the latest official figures.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Full_Length_shot_of_The_Statue_of_Unity.jpg/1920px-Full_Length_shot_of_The_Statue_of_Unity.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail" alt="Statue of Unity. A new Rain Forest attraction at Ekta Nagar near the Statue of Unity is set to be inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi during his October 30-31 visit to Gujarat, according to a Gujarat Chief Minister\'s Office release." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">Statue of Unity. A new Rain Forest attraction at Ekta Nagar near the Statue of Unity is set to be inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi during his October 30-31 visit to Gujarat, according to a Gujarat Chief Minister\'s Office release.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Flora from three regions, in one place</h2>\n<p>According to the official release, the Rain Forest is a one-stop showcase of India\'s diverse evergreen flora. It houses native plant and tree species collected from different ecological zones, including the Western Ghats, the Himalayas, and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands. Thematic landscaping is intended to depict the multi-layered canopy structure typical of tropical rainforests.</p>\n<p>Alongside the skywalk, the site has a waterfall 5 to 8 metres high, a circular pond and connecting bridges. Its entrance gateway, 20 feet high and 40 feet wide, is built in natural stone, and trails use local Narmada stone.</p>\n<p>For younger visitors, there is a children\'s activity zone with a rock-climbing wall, slides, swings and elevated tree balconies linked by stairways.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/THE_STATUE_OF_UNITY.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original" alt="The world\'s tallest statue, Statue of Unity has been created as a tribute to the \'Iron Man of India\', Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel. A new Rain Forest attraction at Ekta Nagar near the Statue of Unity is set to be inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi during his October 30-31 visit to Gujarat, according to a Gujarat Chief Minister\'s Office release." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">The world\'s tallest statue, Statue of Unity has been created as a tribute to the \'Iron Man of India\', Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel. A new Rain Forest attraction at Ekta Nagar near the Statue of Unity is set to be inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi during his October 30-31 visit to Gujarat, according to a Gujarat Chief Minister\'s Office release.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Education as well as tourism</h2>\n<p>A dedicated information centre teaches rainforest ecology, biodiversity and conservation through interactive maps, QR code-enabled displays and visual exhibits. Officials said it will also serve as a field research hub and promote local economic activity.</p>\n<p>The new attraction joins an existing cluster at Ekta Nagar that already includes the Valley of Flowers, Jungle Safari, Cactus Garden, Butterfly Garden, Arogya Van and Vishwa Van. That cluster has been drawing growing numbers. One report on the project noted the Statue of Unity saw 58.25 lakh visitors in 2024, up 13.8 percent from 51.20 lakh in 2023, and that Ekta Nagar recorded 68.3 lakh domestic tourists in 2025, according to <a href="https://tradebrains.in/money/gujarat-plans-12-crore-12-acre-rainforest-attraction-near-statue-of-unity-could-nearby-areas-benefit-12756497" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trade Brains</a>.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Landscapes_of_Western_Ghats_from_Mullayyanagiri_Betta.jpg/1920px-Landscapes_of_Western_Ghats_from_Mullayyanagiri_Betta.jpg" alt="Landscapes of Western Ghats captured on our way to the summit of Mullayyanagiri Betta. The site brings together evergreen flora from the Western Ghats, the Himalayas and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, plus an information centre and a children\'s activity zone." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">Landscapes of Western Ghats captured on our way to the summit of Mullayyanagiri Betta. The site brings together evergreen flora from the Western Ghats, the Himalayas and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, plus an information centre and a children\'s activity zone.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>How diaspora visitors can reach it</h2>\n<p>Ekta Nagar, formerly Kevadia, is built for onward travel once visitors reach Gujarat. The fastest practical route is to fly into Vadodara Airport and drive the 90 kilometres to Kevadia, a journey of roughly two hours from landing to the site entrance, according to a <a href="https://statueofunity.in/how-to-reach-statue-of-unity-travel-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Statue of Unity travel guide</a>. Ahmedabad is about 200 kilometres away by road, roughly 3.5 to 4 hours.</p>\n<p>By rail, direct trains run to Ekta Nagar, also referred to as Kevadia station, from Vadodara, Ahmedabad, Surat and Delhi, and the station is about 5 kilometres from the Statue of Unity entrance. The guide lists the statue site as open Tuesday to Sunday, closed Monday — worth checking, along with the inauguration-week schedule, before travelling.</p>\n<div class="related-coverage"><div class="related-coverage-title">Related coverage</div><a class="related-item" href="/articles/diwali-london-2026-greenwich-dishoom-feasts"><span class="related-item-title">Diwali in London 2026: Greenwich&#x27;s Free Festival, Dishoom and the Feasts</span><span class="related-item-meta">Travel · 2026-10-29</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/india-direct-taiwan-flights-pakistan-airspace"><span class="related-item-title">India Says Direct Taiwan Flights Will Fly Despite Pakistan Airspace Row</span><span class="related-item-meta">Travel · 2026-10-29</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/tawang-international-tourism-mart-2026-buyers-22-countries"><span class="related-item-title">Tawang Hosts International Tourism Mart With Buyers From 22 Countries</span><span class="related-item-meta">Travel · 2026-10-28</span></a></div>', 'slug': 'statue-of-unity-rain-forest-glass-skywalk-ekta-nagar'}, 'inglis': {'headline': 'Josh Inglis Makes Australian History With Comeback Test Century in Cape Town Decider', 'body': '<div class="key-takeaways"><ul><li>Josh Inglis scored 102 off 135 balls in the third Test against South Africa at Newlands, reaching his hundred off 132 deliveries with a reverse sweep.</li><li>He is the first Australian to score multiple centuries in all three international formats — Tests, ODIs and T20Is.</li><li>Inglis was a late recall after Steve Smith was ruled out with a neck injury, and had been recovering from a fractured finger; he had not played a Test in almost a year.</li><li>His stand with Alex Carey (80) took Australia from 191/4 past South Africa\'s 329; Australia were 411/8 at lunch on day three, leading by 82.</li></ul></div>\n<p>Josh Inglis etched his name into Australian cricket history on Thursday, scoring a comeback century in the series-deciding third Test against South Africa at Newlands to become the first Australian with multiple centuries across all three formats of the game.</p>\n<p>Inglis, recalled to the Test XI after nearly a year out, made 102 off 135 balls with 11 fours and two sixes. He reached his hundred off 132 balls with a reverse sweep off Simon Harmer on the third morning, before edging the same bowler behind to wicketkeeper Ryan Rickelton in the same over, according to <a href="https://www.foxsports.com.au/cricket/australia-vs-south-africa-test-live-cricket-scores-third-test-in-cape-town-day-three-josh-inglis-alex-carey-scorecard-news-videos-highlights/news-story/06d0d0e8249feb2fcfdd5f19aadf017d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fox Sports\' live coverage</a>.</p>\n<h2>A record nobody else holds</h2>\n<p>This was Inglis\'s second Test century — his first came on debut against Sri Lanka in Galle last year, when he made 102 off just 90 balls, the second-fastest hundred by a batter on Test debut. The Cape Town ton completes a set no Australian has managed: multiple centuries in Tests, ODIs and T20Is. Glenn Maxwell has five T20I hundreds and four ODI hundreds but only one Test century, against India in 2017. Aaron Finch scored two T20I hundreds and 17 ODI hundreds but never a Test hundred. Inglis now has at least two in each format.</p>\n\nhttps://x.com/SkyCricket/status/2115735164210889080\n\n<h2>Thirty minutes\' notice</h2>\n<p>The innings was unlikely on several levels. Inglis was drafted in at short notice — reportedly about 30 minutes before the match — after Steve Smith was ruled out with a neck injury. He had been recovering from a fractured finger sustained during the preceding ODI series against South Africa, had missed the first two Tests, and had not played competitive red-ball cricket since March. Australia made four changes for the decider, with Cameron Green, Nathan Lyon and Cooper Connolly also returning.</p>\n<p>Walking in with Australia in difficulty after Marco Jansen removed Green and Labuschagne in quick succession, Inglis counter-attacked alongside Alex Carey, who made 80. Their partnership carried Australia past South Africa\'s first-innings 329, and by lunch on day three the visitors were 411 for 8, a lead of 82, with Connolly and Lyon at the crease — the match, and the series, still live.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Ciudad_del_Cabo_desde_Cabeza_de_Le%C3%B3n%2C_Sud%C3%A1frica%2C_2018-07-22%2C_DD_34.jpg/1920px-Ciudad_del_Cabo_desde_Cabeza_de_Le%C3%B3n%2C_Sud%C3%A1frica%2C_2018-07-22%2C_DD_34.jpg" alt="Cape Town viewed from Lion\'s Head, South Africa. Josh Inglis scored 102 off 135 balls in the third Test against South Africa at Newlands, reaching his hundred off 132 deliveries with a reverse sweep." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">Cape Town viewed from Lion\'s Head, South Africa. Josh Inglis scored 102 off 135 balls in the third Test against South Africa at Newlands, reaching his hundred off 132 deliveries with a reverse sweep.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>The IPL connection</h2>\n<p>Indian audiences have their own reference point for Inglis: he was Punjab Kings\' Rs 2.60 crore signing for IPL 2025, and Lucknow Super Giants paid Rs 8.6 crore for him at the IPL 2026 mini-auction despite his signalling limited availability that season. A wicketkeeper-batter who can score a 132-ball Test hundred on recall, in a decider away from home, explains why franchises keep paying for the option.</p>\n\n<figure style="margin:28px auto;text-align:center"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/20251224_Josh_Inglis_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original" alt="Josh Inglis during an Australia practice session at the MCG on 24 December 2025. Josh Inglis scored 102 off 135 balls in the third Test against South Africa at Newlands, reaching his hundred off 132 deliveries with a reverse sweep." style="max-width:100%;border-radius:8px" loading="lazy"><figcaption style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px">Josh Inglis during an Australia practice session at the MCG on 24 December 2025. Josh Inglis scored 102 off 135 balls in the third Test against South Africa at Newlands, reaching his hundred off 132 deliveries with a reverse sweep.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class="related-coverage"><div class="related-coverage-title">Related coverage</div><a class="related-item" href="/articles/happening-2026-10-27-south-africa-cricket-vs-australia-cricket"><span class="related-item-title">South Africa Faces Australia in Crucial ODI at Newlands</span><span class="related-item-meta">Sports · 2026-10-27</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/happening-2026-10-18-south-africa-cricket-vs-australia-cricket"><span class="related-item-title">South Africa vs Australia: Crucial ODI Clash at St George&#x27;s Park</span><span class="related-item-meta">Sports · 2026-10-18</span></a><a class="related-item" href="/articles/dhoni-ipl-2027-decision-before-december-csk-fitness"><span class="related-item-title">Dhoni to Decide on IPL 2027 Before December, With Fitness Tests to Settle CSK Return</span><span class="related-item-meta">Sports · 2026-10-25</span></a></div>', 'slug': 'josh-inglis-australian-history-comeback-test-century-cape-town'}}

